  lendDate=""
  borrowings=""
  favourite="0"
  tags=""
 >
  <description>Arnie 2 is an isometric action game. Although it's obvious to which Hollywood actor the game's name alludes, Arnie 2 is not a licensed title and not based on any movie.

It is the sequel to the Amiga / C64 platform game Arnie (1992). Unlike the previous title it has been developed in-house by Zeppelin

Fast reflexes and a lust for violence are required for success here. Your average muscleman shoots and bombs his way through four enemy-infested territories: Shut down a chemical plant by blowing up enough pipeline valves. Clear an airfield from enemy soldiers, tanks and helicopters. Climb from battleship to battleship in a hostile harbor. And finally, rescue as many prisoners as possible from a jungle POW camp.

Naturally, the levels are crawling with enemies: from simple infantry armed with rifles, mortars or rocket launchers to MG teams, missile batteries and tanks. Most of the opponents are stationary and stolidly fire in a single direction; however, foxholes spew out cannon-fodder at a constant rate. To destroy such nests or efficiently dispatch groups of soldiers, Arnie has a (limited) supply of grenades. Some deceased foes drop more powerful weapons as well as invaluable first aid kits and extra lives.</description>
  <comments></comments>
  <secrets>Hints &amp; Tactics
ARNIE 2 HINTS &amp; TACTICS

(PC VERSION)

by Christian Schmidt, 2002


BASIC TACTICS

* Move carefully. Caution is the key to survival. Never charge into unknown territory. Thoroughly clear any areas you come through; you never know when you may need to retreat there.

* Dont ever let soldiers get too close. They will hurt you on touch. This is most likely to happen if you try to fight multiple enemies at once. Dont. Retreat if a screen crawls. Stand in corners or against walls for protection; this way, enemies cant surround you.

* In each new screen, identify stationary opponents, their direction and frequency of fire. Since all troops (same as you) can only shoot in angles of 45°, its easy to dodge stationary opponents by standing in between their firing lines. Then get them from the side or from behind. This is usually not a problem; your worst enemies are the mobile forces.

* Foxholes contain an unlimited amount of standard soldiers. New arrivals are sitting ducks while they climb out of the hole, so try to shoot them then. As long as theres only one source of infantry on the screen, you can easily keep the soldiers in check until you move on. Be very careful if there are two or more foxholes, however; try to blow them up with a grenade as quickly as possible.

* The round, grey spots in the dirt are land mines. Each level (except for the harbor) has them. Step on one, and it kills you instantly. So you better dont.

* Be careful when approaching narrow bridges. They are usually under heavy fire, and theres no room for you do dodge attacks. Clear the opposite side as good as possible before crossing. Even if a bridge looks safe, move over it quickly and without stopping. Never fight on bridges!

* The program will permanently remove any active enemies which are more than one screen away. To clear enemy lines without a single shot, move into range of sight, then retreat approximately two screens. When you return, all opponents will be gone. Note that this also applies to captives in the POW camp mission, so use this trick with care there.

WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT

* Superior firepower is crucial. Always activate the most powerful gun (RETURN key). The weapons are (from worst to best): Pistol, M1 Rifle, Colt Commando, Uzi, Secret Weapon.

* If you die, you lose the currently active weapon. You dont want that to happen. So when your health drops close to zero, switch to the pistol. After you lost your life, switch back to the most powerful gun.

* Dont waste grenades on normal enemies. You need them to blow up foxholes and the occasional tank. Arnie throws grenades farther in a diagonal direction, so aim carefully.

* First aid kits dont disappear. Dont take them if your health is good. Return to pick them up when youre heavily wounded.

ENEMY DESCRIPTIONS AND TACTICS

Soldiers

Standard mobile enemy. Killed with one shot, but dangerous through sheer number. Soldier dont aim well, but are sometimes astonishingly persistent in following you. When fighting multiple soldiers at once, back away or hide in a corner, then wait for them to step into your line of fire.

MG teams

Stationary, fixed direction. Fire bursts of three projectiles in short intervals. Protected by sandbag barriers. Can be shot at the right corner (aim carefully), else kill with grenade or ignore. 

Mortars

Stationary, fixed direction. Mortars alternately fire one close and one far grenade. Easy to dodge, but do a lot of damage if they hit. Sometimes protected by sandbag barriers.

Snipers

Stationary, fixed direction. Snipers hide in houses, behind rocks or other objects, depending on the scenario. They leave cover in varying intervals to fire single shots. No problem if you attack from the side, but beware that some are impossible to hit as long as they take cover.

Rocket launchers

Stationary, fixed direction. They fire single rockets that hurt a lot if they hit. Luckily, they are easily avoided and killed from the side. Very troublesome when they cover narrow bridges. In such cases, line up with the rocket launcher between shots, fire, then quickly step aside to dodge the next missile. Repeat until the enemy is dead.

Grenadiers

Limited mobility and throwing range. Grenadiers patrol trenches and throw grenades to the left and right. They dont aim, but do a lot of damage if they hit. Only vulnerably while they prepare to throw. Line up with trench and fire when a grenadier sticks his head out.

Tanks

Limited mobility, fixed direction. Tanks are nothing much else than a bigger rocket launcher, easily destroyed despite their size. Some move a short distance when you first see them, but are immobile once they stop. Three grenades will wipe out a tank, but thats a waste of ammo. Either shoot until it explodes, or simply ignore it.

Manned tanks

Limited mobility and direction of fire. Same as standard tanks, but with a soldier on top that shoots in any direction. Though you may hit and kill the soldier separately, it will die when the tank explodes.

MG trucks

Stationary, free direction of fire. Fires single shots in your direction. Rare and not very dangerous. Destroy with grenades.

Missile batteries

Stationary, free direction of fire. Fires small missiles in your direction in quick succession. Hard to kill without being hit (throw a grenade) unless you approach from below. Move to the left or right of the battery and close up to the line of projectiles, then shoot at the base.

Helicopters

Limited mobility, fixed direction. Flies straight across the screen once, firing small missiles. Indestructible, but gone once it leaves the screen. Simply avoid.
</secrets>
  <genre>
   <line>
    <col>Action</col>
   </line>
   <line>
    <col>Action/Shoot'em'up</col>
   </line>
  </genre>
 </item>
